    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Nissan Returns to the Annual SEMA Show Featuring a 300-HP Sentra Spec-V Revival Concept, Two Z Cars\nVauxhall Passes 100,000 Electric Vehicle Sales, Two Models Account for Nearly Half\n12 photos\nPhoto: Vauxhall\nStill twinned with Opel, the Vauxhall brand is celebrating a very important milestone. In the span of a few short years, it managed to sell precisely 102,852 electric vehicles in the United Kingdom. Its battery-electric journey started in 2019 with the electric version of the Corsa, which originally had 136 ponies and 50 kilowatt-hours to its name.\nThe current model packs a bigger punch, one additional kilowatt-hour, and more driving range to boot, specifically 265 miles (426 kilometers) instead of 209 miles (336 kilometers) in the WLTP. According to Vauxhall, a customer who purchased an electric Grandland from Drive Vauxhall Nuneaton pushed cumulative BEV sales past six figures.\nAlthough the Grandland is a great compact model, the smaller Mokka Electric and the Corsa Electric have done most of the heavy lifting so far. The Mokka Electric leads the lineup with more than 26,000 registrations since 2020, whereas the Corsa Electric totals 23,000 units. Cumulative sales reached 10,000 vehicles in 2021, then passed 50,000 in 2023.\nVauxhall spent years building a broad electric vehicle lineup, with the British marque completing its commitment to offer electric versions of every passenger car in the range by the end of 2024. The current selection includes the Astra twins, Frontera , Combo Life, and Vivaro Life.\nNaturally, the commercial vehicle side of the business also deserves attention. Vauxhall started offering an electric version of every van in 2021, and better still, it was crowned Britain's most popular electric van brand for three consecutive years. The Combo Electric is produced locally, with Vauxhall identifying Ellesmere Port as the UK's first volume manufacturing plant dedicated exclusively to battery-electric vehicles.\nPhoto: Vauxhall Going back in time, the British company's electric ambitions are rooted in the range-extender technology introduced by the Ampera back in the 2010s. Mechanically related to the Opel Ampera, Chevrolet Volt, Holden Volt, and the Cadillac ELR, the OG hides an internal combustion engine underhood.\nSaid engine does not directly power the wheels under normal conditions. It usually turns on to spin a generator that recharges the traction battery. Three clutches lock or unlock the planet carrier, sun gear, and ring gear to shift between purely electric and range-extended operation. The internal combustion engine drives the front wheels only at higher vehicle speeds, when the battery is depleted.\nBetween the Vauxhall Ampera and the full-electric Corsa, the Ampera-e was supposed to serve as the brand's first mass-market electric vehicle. While its Opel-branded sibling enjoyed limited European commercial success, General Motors ultimately canceled the right-hand-drive Vauxhall model before its planned UK market release.\nTurning our attention back to the present day, Vauxhall has also expanded its electric vehicle lineup with high-performance versions. The Mokka GSE and Corsa GSE come to mind, with prices currently starting at £36,170 and £34,495, respectively, OTR. By comparison, the most affordable full-electric Corsa on sale today in the United Kingdom is advertised from £26,780 by its maker.\n\"From the revolutionary Ampera to today’s fully electrified car and van range, we have remained a champion of EVs, and our focus is to continue making electric mobility more accessible and attainable for British drivers and businesses,\" said Eurig Druce, Vauxhall Managing Director and Stellantis UK Group Managing Director.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVauxhall UK EV sales production\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Man Buys New 2024 Corvette Z06 Convertible for $152,415, Drives 1,200 Miles, Sells for $115,000",
